<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Normal distributions</title>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let's begin with the function
        <md>
          p(x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}e^{-x^2/2},
        </md>
        which defines a ``normal distribution.''
        Plot this function and verify that it has the
        shape of a bell curve centered about the point <m>x=0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        One inconvenience in dealing with this function is that it does not
        have an antiderivative in terms of functions that are familiar to
        us.  Therefore, any definite integrals that we would like to
        evaluate involving this function require the use of a numerical
        method.  That means that you'll need to use Desmos to evaluate any
        definite integrals of this function.
      </p>
    </introduction>
      
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Since this is a probability density function, what should be
          the value of
          <md>
            \int_{-\infty}^\infty p(x)~dx?
          </md>
          Verify that this is the case.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="23">
        <p>
          Using Desmos, we find that
          <md>
            \int_{-\infty}^\infty p(x)~dx = 1
          </md>
          as required for a probability density function.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the probability that a data point will lie between
          <m>0</m> and <m>1</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="23">
        <p>
          Using Desmos again,
          <md>
            \int_0^1 \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}e^{-x^2/2}~dx \approx 0.341
          </md>
          so about 34.1%.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose that we measure the heights of men in the United States and
        find the normal distribution
        <md>
          p(x)=\frac1{3\sqrt{2\pi}}e^{-(x-70)^2/18}
        </md>
        where <m>x</m> is measured in inches.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Plot this function (what is a reasonable range of <m>x</m>
          values?) and discuss the significance of the graph.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="23">
        <p>
          A reasonable range is roughly <m>55 \leq x \leq 85</m>.
          The graph is a bell curve centered at <m>x=70</m> inches,
          indicating that the average height is 70 inches, with a
          spread of a few inches on either side.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the average height.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="23">
        <p>
          <md>
            \overline{x} = \int_{-\infty}^\infty x\cdot p(x)~dx = 70
          </md>
          inches, the center of the distribution.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Then find the fraction of men whose height is between 70 and 72
          inches.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="23">
        <p>
          <md>
            \int_{70}^{72} p(x)~dx \approx 0.248
          </md>
          so about 24.8%.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What fraction of men are taller than 74 inches?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="23">
        <p>
          <md>
            \int_{74}^\infty p(x)~dx \approx 0.091
          </md>
          so about 9.1%.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the median height?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="23">
        <p>
          The cumulative distribution function is
          <md>
            C(s)=\int_{-\infty}^s p(x)~dx
          </md>
          and we want the median <m>m</m> that satisfies
          <m>C(m)=1/2</m> so <m>m=70</m> again.  The median is the
          same as the average or mean.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>\overline{x}</m> is the mean of the density function,
          the standard
          deviation <m>\sigma</m>, which measures the spread in the
          data, is given
          by
          <md>
            \sigma^2 = \int_{-\infty}^\infty (x-\overline{x})^2p(x)~dx.
          </md>
          Notice that this is defining <m>\sigma^2</m> so you'll need
          to take the
          square root of the integral.  Find the standard deviation of this
          distribution.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="23">
        <p>
          <md>
            \sigma^2 = \int_{-\infty}^\infty (x-70)^2 p(x)~dx = 9
          </md>
          so <m>\sigma = 3</m> inches.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The general form of the normal distribution is
        <md>
          p(x) = \frac{1}{\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}}
          e^{-\frac{(x-\bar{x})^2}{2\sigma^2}}
        </md>.
        Average annual snowfall in Grand Rapids is 72.4 inches with a standard
        deviation of 16 inches.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A few years ago, we had a lot of snow with 109
          inches recorded.  What is the chance that there is more than 109
          inches of snow?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="23">
        <p>
          <md>
            \int_{109}^\infty p(x)~dx \approx 0.011
          </md>
          so about 1.1%.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the
          chance that there is 40 or fewer inches of snow in a year?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="23">
        <p>
          <md>
            \int_{-\infty}^{40} p(x)~dx \approx 0.021
          </md>
          so about 2.1%.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the chance that the snowfall is more than the average?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="23">
        <p>
          By the symmetry of the normal distribution,
          <md>
            \int_{72.4}^\infty p(x)~dx = 0.5
          </md>
          so 50%.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the chance that the snowfall is more than one standard
          deviation above the average?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="23">
        <p>
          One standard deviation above the mean is <m>72.4 + 16 =
          88.4</m> inches.
          <md>
            \int_{88.4}^\infty p(x)~dx \approx 0.159
          </md>
          so about 15.9%.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
